p=O/'physics-development.html';s=p.read_text(encoding='utf-8-sig');s=s.replace('</html>','''<section id="quadratic-brake-law"><h2>Testing nonlinear differential-brake moments</h2><p>Slegers and Costello's <a href="https://digitalcommons.georgefox.edu/mece_fac/3/">Aspects of Control for a Parafoil and Payload System (2003)</a> describes competing roll- and skid-steering responses and sensitivity to brake magnitude, canopy curvature and incidence. The publisher-hosted abstract was available; the linked full paper returned an access error. This motivates a nonlinear-response test, not a claim that the HobbyKing canopy underwent a steering-mode transition.</p><pre>bL = max(left_pull − left_onset, 0) / 0.072
bR = max(right_pull − right_onset, 0) / 0.072
Δb = bR − bL; Δb² = bR² − bL²
Cl_brake = Clδ Δb + Cl2 Δb²
Cn_brake = Cnδ Δb + Cn2 Δb²</pre><p>The quadratic difference is shorthand for the difference of squares, not the square of the differential input. It adds two coefficients to the existing two-body model. All 62 training segments, weights and 254 excluded comparisons are unchanged. Forty randomized checks confirm exact recovery of the original model when the new coefficients are zero, no added moment for symmetric braking, and correct total force/moment balance to floating-point precision.</p><figure><img src="iteration-quadratic-brake.png" alt="Quadratic brake-moment law, excluded bank errors and Hood River turn entry"><figcaption>The coefficient curve is the fitted model's contribution after engagement offsets, not a measured aerodynamic curve. Its large-pull behavior is unvalidated extrapolation. The right panel is a free prediction of a repeatedly inspected development turn.</figcaption></figure><p>The four excluded high-bank Trout Lake segments improve modestly: median bank error falls from 17.35 to 15.85 degrees and median absolute final heading error from 61.20 to 46.85 degrees. Ordinary-flight errors generally worsen slightly. The Hood River 450-second turn still has 19.58 degrees bank RMS error and −82.6 degrees final heading error. The model is rejected. Its fitted quadratic roll term is negative, so it does not support the initial guess that stronger input simply supplies more roll moment across the range.</p><p>Evidence: <code>quadratic-brake-mechanics-checks.json</code>, <code>quadratic-brake-body-fit.json</code>, <code>quadratic-brake-comparison.json</code>. Reproduction: <code>fast_quadratic_brake.cpp</code>, <code>test_quadratic_brake.py</code>, <code>fit_quadratic_brake_body.py</code>.</p><h3>Sampling and sensor audit</h3><p>Both flights contain IMU instance 0 and EKF core 0, consistent with EK3_IMU_MASK=1 and EK3_PRIMARY=0. Logged EKF gyro biases stay below one degree per second. No alternate logged IMU/core mismatch was found.</p><p>A qualification to the earlier “causal initialization” wording: the inference used only preceding grid samples, but those grid samples included linear interpolation of raw attitude, gyro and throttle records. Such interpolation can use a following raw sample within one logging interval. A new observation stream now uses only the latest raw sample at or before each grid time, with its source timestamp retained. Maximum observed attitude and control ages are about 40 ms; gyro age is about 20 ms. The brake-travel series is identical because it already used zero-order hold.</p><p>A frozen-parameter replay with this strictly past-sample stream changes bank RMS errors by at most 0.18 degrees and final heading errors by at most 3.2 degrees across the six development cases. It does not change the model rejection. Future strict validation should use this new stream, keeping interpolation only for the scored reference when appropriate. Evidence: <code>imu-core-audit.json</code>, <code>causal-resampling-audit.json</code>, <code>strict-sampling-body-check.json</code>; reproducible scripts: <code>audit_causal_resampling.py</code>, <code>check_strict_sampling_body.py</code>.</p></section></html>''');p.write_text(s,encoding='utf-8')
